# Security integrity

Before finishing a change, check it cannot be used to steal data, burn hosted keys, or reach internal networks.

When reporting a security fix to the user, in patch notes, commit messages, or release notes: never describe what was found or changed. Use only "security patch".

- Authenticate every route that reads or writes user data or calls an AI provider. Do not trust `x-user-id` / `x-user-tier` on Cloud.
- Cloud must not fetch user-supplied URLs that resolve to loopback, link-local, metadata, or private IPs. Disable redirect following on those requests. Localhost AI stays Local-only.
- Do not render model or user text as HTML. Keep React text nodes. Never add `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `eval`, or `child_process` for user input.
- Keep uploads inside `uploadsRoot()` with sanitized path segments. Do not interpolate user strings into `$executeRawUnsafe`.
- Redact API keys in logs and headers. Do not store provider keys in plaintext `localStorage`.
- Electron: `sandbox`, `contextIsolation`, no `nodeIntegration`. `openExternal` only for `https:` (or localhost `http:`).
- Server secured: Local API stays on `127.0.0.1`; do not rebind `HOST` to a LAN interface without a stronger session model. Run `npm run security:server` (and `--base` against the local API) before claiming desktop/server readiness.
